artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let $M$ be a smooth manifold. A Lorentzian metric on $M$ is a smooth, non-degenerate symmetric $(0,2)$-tensor field of signature $(-,+,\ldots,+)$, while a Riemannian metric on $M$ is a smooth, positive-definite symmetric $(0,2)$-tensor field. The classical Wick rotation is the procedure of analytically continuing the time coordinate $t \mapsto i\tau$ in a static Lorentzian metric of the form $g = -dt^2 + h$ (with $h$ a Riemannian metric on a spatial slice) so as to obtain the Riemannian metric $g_E = d\tau^2 + h$.

Formulate and prove a generalization of the Wick rotation that applies beyond the static case. Specifically, identify intrinsic geometric, topological, and/or analytic conditions on a Lorentzian manifold $(M,g_L)$—possibly involving the existence of a distinguished timelike vector field, a foliation by spacelike hypersurfaces, an analytic structure, curvature constraints, or an embedding into a complexification—under which there exists a canonical procedure producing a Riemannian metric $g_R$ on $M$ (or on a related manifold) such that:
(i) the construction reduces to the standard Wick rotation in the static case;
(ii) the correspondence $g_L \leftrightarrow g_R$ is well-defined and, in an appropriate sense, natural (for instance, compatible with diffeomorphisms and with analytic continuation of curvature invariants);
(iii) the conditions are sharp, in that a Lorentzian metric admits such a "Wick rotation" to a Riemannian metric if and only if the stated conditions hold.

Determine necessary and sufficient conditions on $(M,g_L)$ for the existence of such a generalized Wick rotation.

